  {
    "rank": 1,
    "pattern_title": "Authorization and facility mapping issues are distorting inpatient and post-acute spend signals",
    "pattern_description": "Before taking utilization or network action, the plan needs to validate how authorization status and facility type are being assigned on claims. A large share of the increase sits in authorization-code redistribution and Not Mapped facility buckets across GA, CA, ME / NY / OH, and CT / NH / NY / OH. That means some apparent provider, site-of-care, or authorization trends may be classification movement rather than true operating change.",
    "explanation": {
      "reimbursement": {
        "summary_table": {},
        "individual_policies": []
      },
      "claims": {
        "summary": "GA authorization-code movement added about $2.2M, led by Aftercare with CC/MCC at about $1.3M and Kidney and Urinary Tract Infections without MCC at about $0.9M. CA authorization-code movement added about $0.9M in Miscellaneous Disorders of Nutrition, Metabolism, and Fluids and Electrolytes with MCC. Not Mapped facility claims added about $0.3M in CA, about $0.3M in GA, and about $0.3M across CT / NH / NY / OH, with concentration at Good Samaritan Hospital and AdventHealth Palm Coast Parkway."
      }
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "pattern_title": "CA spend is rising in acute hospital and rehab settings, concentrated in aftercare, electrolyte, trauma, and spine cases",
    "pattern_description": "CA has a concentrated inpatient and post-acute cost increase across PPO Local acute hospital and physical rehab settings. The operational increase is reinforced by clinical concentration in aftercare, electrolyte disorders, and a smaller set of rare trauma and spine cases, with Good Samaritan Hospital appearing repeatedly. This looks less like broad-based inflation and more like a focused mix of placement, case complexity, and provider concentration.",
    "explanation": {
      "reimbursement": {
        "summary_table": {},
        "individual_policies": []
      },
      "claims": {
        "summary": "CA physical rehab added about $0.7M in mixed volume and unit-cost pressure, including 10 more admissions and about $16.0K higher paid per admit in Aftercare with CC/MCC. CA acute hospital added about $0.8M in mixed and volume-led pressure, led by Aftercare, Musculoskeletal System and Connective Tissue with CC at about $0.3M and Kidney and Urinary Tract Infections without MCC at about $0.2M. Clinical case mix added about $0.9M from aftercare and electrolyte diagnoses, including ENC AFTERCARE FLW SURGERY NEOPLASM at about $0.3M and HYPO-OSMOLALITY AND HYPONATREMIA at about $0.2M. Rare trauma and spine-related cases added about $0.9M, including several single-case episodes paid at roughly $40K to $175K per admit."
      }
    }
  },
  {
